el is an average of both."�As a general rule, the types of Fused Pokémon are determined by its head's first type and its body's second type (if it's a dual type Pokémon)."�For example, a fusion of Butterfree (Bug-Flying) as the head with Tentacool (Water-Poison) as the body will be a Bug/Poison type Pokémon."XBut some Pokémon, such as evolved starter Pokémon always pass their primary type."HBoth halves of fused Pokémon evolve independantly from each other,"TEach half will evolve whenever the corresponding unfused Pokémon would evolve."BFused Pokémon naturally learn moves from both base Pokémon."hThe stats of fused Pokémon are determined by a weighed average of the stats of both base Pokémon."TThe Attack, Defense and Speed stats are closer to those of its body's Pokémon."eAnd the HP, Special Attack and Special Defense stats are closer to those of its head's Pokémon."sFused Pokémon get to choose between the body Pokémon's first ability and the head Pokémon's second ability."FThe one it gets can be chosen after fusing two Pokémon together."XIf you use DNA Splicers on an already fused Pokémon, the two Pokémon will unfuse."~Both of your Pokémon will revert to their original levels, but any experience they gained while they were fused is preserved."2Would you like to learn about something else?"6Do you want to know something cool about berries?"�When a Pokémon eats a berry it's holding, it will automatically hold another one after the battle if you have more in your bag."Try it out yourself!"$And wear this one on your head!"Pftt... Your loss!"mPokémon will automatically re-hold any consumable item after they're consumed if you have more of them!"Yes"No[."nThere's a notice on the board: \C[2]Some of the information in this classroom might be slightly outdated."=\C[2]Check the Wiki for the most up-to-date information!"E\PN acquired some knowledge by looking intensively at the globe.")Hi! Welcome to the Pokémon Academy!"3Would you like to learn about Pokémon Fusion?"